// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/turbobaby/delivery_terms.t27 -- the delivery promise: one island, two handover points,
; a published tariff ladder of twelve districts, and a dash for every district off it. Since
; 2026-09-24 also the zone table the shop ships: the owner's eighteen Phuket zones, and no ETA.
; This contract owns the shape of what the shop promises about getting a bike to a client:
; the coverage rule, the handover point and the agreement it needs, the window geometry read
; off the wall clock, and the published area tariff. It is the named PRODUCER of the
; `delivery_destination_ready` boolean that turbobaby/commerce consumes abstractly in its own
; declaration commerce_fulfillment_valid: commerce decides whether an order may be delivered,
; this file decides only whether the destination leg is complete.
; THE EDGE IS NAMED BY OWNER AND DECLARATION, NOT BY LINE (corrected 2026-09-21). Until this edit
; it cited commerce.t27 by a line range, 146 through 156. That range was re-read on the day it was
; deleted and it still held -- which is the argument against it and not for it: commerce.t27 grew
; from 411 lines to 855 between the two readings, and the citation survived only because every
; addition happened to land past the end of the range. A number that is right by luck reads
; exactly like a number that is right. The old form is described here rather than quoted, so that
; a sweep for line citations into a sibling does not find this paragraph and count it as one.
; What was re-verified instead is the sentence: turbobaby/commerce declares
; commerce_fulfillment_valid, delivery_destination_ready is its fourth argument, and its delivery
; arm still returns the conjunction of that boolean with the upstream delivery_allowed.
; Transcribed from data/fleet_seed.json rental_terms.delivery, lines 295-309 (measured_at
; 2026-09-12, schema_version 2). TWO SOURCES SINCE 2026-09-21: the coverage rule, the handover
; points and the window geometry come from that seed, and the tariff ladder and the delivery
; cut-off come from the owner's knowledge base, cited as brain:<node>:<line> and TRANSLATED from
; Russian. SOURCE below still names the seed, because it names the repository-tracked file a gate
; could bind to, and nothing in this tree can resolve a brain citation. ASCII only (L3), English
; identifiers only -- no Cyrillic reaches this file from the brain, not even inside a comment.
; --
; WHY (measured 2026-09-20). Delivery is the FOURTH topic by client volume in the corpus behind
; KB_faq -- 499 messages of the ten topics counted in data/fleet_seed.json:341 -- and the
; repository answers it from a table that describes a different island. src/delivery.rs ships 5
; default zones (src/delivery.rs:37-78): 4 named places and 1 handover row, and its own header
; (src/delivery.rs:7-20) says the places are villages on Koh Phangan while the shop is in
; Kamala, Phuket. ZERO of the 4 place rows lie inside the coverage area this file pins; the fifth
; row is the shop's own counter, which is inside it and is not a destination. The one delivery
; figure the SEED documents -- 290 THB to a single area, data/fleet_seed.json:306 -- is
; in no table the code reads: `tariff_table` is null (data/fleet_seed.json:307) and the seed's
; own note (line 308) forbids interpolating any other area's price from that one figure. So the
; seed prices one row, the shipped tariff has five wrong ones, and nothing in the repository
; states the rule that reconciles them. That rule is what this file is.
; AMENDED 2026-09-21: the shop's tariff was never in this repository to be found. It is published
; in the owner's knowledge base, by district, and this file now carries it -- see THE PUBLISHED
; TARIFF LADDER below. The sentence above is kept because it is still true of the repository, and
; because it is the measurement that made the ladder worth going to look for.
; CORRECTED 2026-09-24, by the owner's decisions in chat that day (cited as owner, 2026-09-24). The
; shipped zone table no longer describes another island: migrations/087_delivery_zones_phuket.sql
; deactivates the four Koh Phangan rows -- deactivated, not deleted, because placed orders
; reference them -- and inserts the owner's eighteen Phuket zones, and src/delivery.rs carries the
; same eighteen as its built-in table behind the shop's own pickup row. No zone carries an ETA.
; The paragraph above stays as the 2026-09-20 measurement it was; THE SHIPPED ZONE TABLE section
; below says what the table is now, where each row comes from, and how it meets the ladder.
; --
; WHERE THE OBVIOUS READING IS REFUTED. src/api/orders.rs:2541 and :1920 serve the zone fee
; through `finite_money`, with a comment (orders.rs:2537-2540) saying an undocumented fee comes
; back as null and renders as a dash rather than as free delivery. Read alone, that comment says
; the dash already protects the delivery leg. It does not, for three measured reasons.
;   (1) The branch cannot fire. `fee` is NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (fee >= 0) in
;       migrations/060_delivery_zones.sql:6 and `f64` -- not `Option<f64>` -- in
;       src/db/entities/delivery_zone.rs:13, and the admin write path maps every non-finite or
;       negative input to 0.0 (src/api/admin.rs:1038-1044, used at :1084 and :1131-1133). Every
;       row this repository can create therefore carries a finite number, so finite_money returns
;       Some for all of them. An unpriced area is not a null fee; it is a row that does not exist.
;   (2) The client cannot receive the null anyway. src/ui/api/types.rs:405 types
;       `delivery_fee_baht` as a bare `f64`, so a null in that field fails the whole response.
;   (3) When the zone list arrives EMPTY the checkout screen prints a zero fee and a hard-coded
;       "30-45" minute ETA (src/ui/screens/checkout_screen.rs:828-835) through format_baht, which
;       takes `f64` and has no absent case (src/trios/pricing.rs:417, was :361). It is the empty list and not
;       an unselected picker that reaches this branch, because :797 falls back to the first zone
;       whenever one exists. That is the confident zero D9 forbids, still live on the client half.
;       CORRECTED 2026-09-24: the "30-45" literal is gone. No ETA is published (owner, 2026-09-24),
;       and the checkout prints an ETA line only when the zone row carries both minutes, which no
;       shipped row does. The zero FEE on an empty list is unchanged and still the zero D9 forbids
;       (src/ui/screens/checkout_screen.rs:831-835). Re-measured the same day: the fallback to the
;       first zone is at :816 (this paragraph said :797, a reading of an older tree),
;       delivery_fee_baht is at src/ui/api/types.rs:407 (:405 before two ETA attributes landed above
;       it), and the admin write stores the fee at src/api/admin.rs:1130 on create and :1173-1175 on
;       update (:1084 and :1131-1133 before); sanitize_f64 is still :1038-1044.
; The prohibition therefore cannot be a render rule here. Until 2026-09-21 it was the LENGTH of a
; one-element fee array, pinned by an invariant that a second row could not be added without an
; assert being deleted on purpose. That length is now a MEASUREMENT -- the extent of the ladder
; the owner publishes, twelve districts -- and the prohibition has moved to where it belongs: a
; district OFF the ladder yields FEE_ABSENT, and no function in this file maps an unlisted
; district, a distance, or a neighbouring district's price to a number.
; --
; WHAT THIS FILE NEVER DOES. It holds no ETA, no address, no Maps link and no customer (D14) --
; not in a constant, not in a test fixture, not in a comment. It holds a tariff ladder as of
; 2026-09-21 and this sentence used to deny that too; district names and published fees are
; neither D14 data nor a quote, and the clause was narrowed rather than quietly dropped. It does not decide
; whether an order may be delivered: that is admissibility and commerce.t27 owns it. It adds no
; fee to any total, and no function here returns a sum. It does not render an absent amount:
; pricing_honesty.t27 owns the dash and the sentence that must travel with it, and this file only
; reports that the amount is absent. It declares no fulfillment mode, no timezone and no UTC
; offset; each is named by the ID of its owner instead. It does not transcribe the shipped zone
; names: they are Cyrillic in src/delivery.rs:40-72 and in migrations/071_delivery_zones_koh_
; phangan.sql:25-29, L3 forbids Cyrillic in a spec, and they are cited by path and never quoted.
; CORRECTED 2026-09-24: the Russian names are now those of the owner's Phuket zones
; (src/delivery.rs:58-75, migrations/087_delivery_zones_phuket.sql:70-87), still cited by path and
; never quoted. Their ENGLISH names are ASCII and the owner's own spelling, and are declared below
; as PHUKET_ZONE_NAMES_EN so that a gate can bind them. It still holds no ETA: none is published.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module turbobaby-delivery-terms;

pub const KIND : str = "contract";
pub const ID : str = "turbobaby/delivery-terms";
pub const NAME : str = "TurboBaby delivery coverage, window and published fee";
pub const SOURCE : str = "data/fleet_seed.json";
pub const SOURCE_MEASURED_AT : str = "2026-09-12";
pub const MEASURED_AT : str = "2026-09-20";

; --- Reuse edges ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Current t27c does not support brace imports, so an ownership edge is a string naming the owner
; and an assert that pins the literal. That keeps the edge greppable and impossible to delete
; quietly, which an import comment would not be.
; --
; pricing-honesty owns what a reader does with an absent amount: SHAPE_DASH, the DASH sentinel and
; the obligation that the dash never travels without the sentence. This file produces the absence
; and copies none of that rendering machinery. It does declare its own absence sentinel, FEE_ABSENT
; below, and the relationship is named rather than left to be discovered: pricing-honesty's
; NOT_AN_AMOUNT_THB_DAY is the same -1 over a different quantity (a daily rate, typed i16). Neither
; value can be imported into the other -- t27c has no imports -- so the two are separate
; declarations on purpose, and each is justified from its own CHECK constraint.
pub const REUSES_PRICING_HONESTY_ID : str = "turbobaby/pricing-honesty";
; market owns the wall clock the hours below are read on -- TH_TIMEZONE and TH_UTC_OFFSET_HOURS.
; Every minute-of-day constant here is local time on that profile, and this file declares neither
; the zone name nor the offset.
pub const REUSES_MARKET_PROFILE_ID : str = "turbobaby/market";
; commerce owns the fulfillment modes (FULFILLMENT_PICKUP, FULFILLMENT_DELIVERY) and their
; validity. This file declares no mode. The handover points below are a different set answering a
; different question -- WHICH shop point, not WHICH mode -- and they are never a second copy.
pub const REUSES_COMMERCE_ID : str = "turbobaby/commerce";
; order-money owns the total identity -- subtotal less bonus less star discount, floored at zero,
; src/api/orders.rs:210 -- and the tolerance around it. The two constants below about totals say
; only where the delivery fee is NOT, and the identity itself is not restated here in any form a
; caller could evaluate.
pub const REUSES_ORDER_MONEY_ID : str = "turbobaby/order-money";
; The single boolean this contract exists to produce, spelled exactly as the consumer's argument.
pub const PRODUCES : str = "delivery_destination_ready";
pub const CONSUMER_FUNCTION : str = "commerce_fulfillment_valid";

; --- Coverage: one island, and off it the promise ends ------------------------------------------
; data/fleet_seed.json:296. Off the island there is no delivery at all -- not a longer window and
; not a larger fee -- and the client collects the bike. That is a boundary, not a price.
pub const COVERAGE_ISLAND : str = "Phuket";
pub const COVERAGE_ISLAND_COUNT : i32 = 1;
pub const OFF_ISLAND_MEANS_NO_DELIVERY : bool = true;
; The seed says only that the client collects the bike themselves; it names no point for that
; collection, so none is named here either.
pub const OFF_ISLAND_FALLBACK : str = "the client collects the bike themselves";
; A neighbour fact that reads like this one and is not it. data/fleet_seed.json rental_terms.
; off_island permits RIDING to other islands and provinces, with recovery at the client's cost if
; the bike breaks down. That is about where a rented bike may go; coverage is about where the shop
; will bring one. Confusing the two would turn a permission into delivery of a bike to Krabi.
pub const OFF_ISLAND_TRAVEL_IS_A_DIFFERENT_FACT : bool = true;

; --- Handover points ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; data/fleet_seed.json:297-298. The default point is the office. The secondary point exists only
; "by explicit agreement", so the discriminant alone never settles it, and an unknown point fails
; closed rather than falling back to the default.
pub const POINT_NONE : u8 = 0;
pub const POINT_DEFAULT : u8 = 1;
pub const POINT_SECONDARY : u8 = 2;
pub const POINT_NAMES : [2]str = ["default", "secondary"];
pub const DEFAULT_POINT : str = "Kamala office";
pub const SECONDARY_POINT : str = "Bangtao / Thalang";
pub const SECONDARY_POINT_REQUIRES_EXPLICIT_AGREEMENT : bool = true;
; The seed writes the secondary point as ONE field naming two places, and this file does not split
; it into two points: the agreement is per handover, and inventing a second discriminant would
; publish a distinction the source does not make.
pub const SECONDARY_POINT_IS_ONE_SOURCE_FIELD : bool = true;
; data/fleet_seed.json:305. The client ALWAYS gets a map link to the handover point, so the link
; is part of the promise and not a courtesy: the readiness function below refuses without it, the
; same way pricing-honesty refuses a dash that travels without its sentence. No link is stored
; here, and none could be: a link is per-order data (D14).
pub const MUST_SEND_MAP_LINK : bool = true;
pub const NO_MAP_LINK_IS_STORED_IN_THIS_FILE : bool = true;

; --- Window geometry, in minutes of the local day -----------------------------------------------
; Local time on the market profile (REUSES_MARKET_PROFILE_ID). Minutes since local midnight,
; because a clock string is not arithmetic and this language pins integers.
; data/fleet_seed.json:299 office_opens 10:00 -> 600. :300 earliest_window 11:00-13:00 -> 660..780.
; :302 default_window_hours 2 -> 120. :303 narrow_window_hours 1 -> 60. :304 the narrow window is
; "on request, rarely", so it is published but never the default.
pub const MINUTES_PER_HOUR : i32 = 60;
pub const OFFICE_OPENS_MIN : i32 = 600;
pub const EARLIEST_WINDOW_START_MIN : i32 = 660;
pub const EARLIEST_WINDOW_END_MIN : i32 = 780;
pub const DEFAULT_WINDOW_MIN : i32 = 120;
pub const NARROW_WINDOW_MIN : i32 = 60;
pub const WINDOW_LENGTHS_MIN : [2]i32 = [120, 60];
pub const NARROW_WINDOW_IS_BY_REQUEST_ONLY : bool = true;
; data/fleet_seed.json:301: same-day delivery is two hours after the booking is completed, "but
; never before the 11:00-13:00 window". Two rules, and the second one is a floor under the first.
pub const SAME_DAY_LEAD_MIN : i32 = 120;
pub const SAME_DAY_HAS_A_FLOOR : bool = true;
; The hour between opening and the earliest window is measured, not assumed: 660 - 600. A reader
; who expects delivery from the moment the office opens is wrong by exactly this much.
pub const OPENING_TO_EARLIEST_WINDOW_MIN : i32 = 60;
; TWO LIMITS OF THE WINDOW MODEL, DECLARED RATHER THAN HIDDEN, because each one makes a function
; below answer a question narrower than its name suggests.
; (a) The lead is the SAME-DAY rule and nothing else. data/fleet_seed.json:301 attaches the two
;     hours to same-day delivery only, and every quantity here is a minute of ONE local day, so
;     window_start_min and booking_completed_min are minutes of the same day by construction. A
;     delivery on a later day is outside this model: the functions below have no date argument and
;     no calendar, and a caller that feeds them a next-day window will get a refusal the shop's own
;     rule does not make. What is missing is a date, which would have to come with the timezone
;     turbobaby/market owns; no next-day default was invented in either direction.
pub const DELIVERY_DATE_IS_NOT_MODELLED : bool = true;
pub const SAME_DAY_MODEL_NOTE : str = "Every minute-of-day quantity in this file belongs to one local day, so window_start_is_allowed and window_shape_is_published answer the SAME-DAY question only. data/fleet_seed.json:301 scopes the two-hour lead to same-day delivery; nothing in the repository says what the lead is for a later day, and the seed publishes no booking-to-delivery calendar. Until a date model exists, a consumer must apply these two functions to a same-day window and must not read a refusal from them as the shop refusing a next-day one.";
; (b) There is no closing time. data/fleet_seed.json:299 publishes office_opens and no counterpart,
;     and no source anywhere in the repository states when the day ends, so nothing below puts an
;     upper bound on a window start. A late hour is therefore unjudged rather than permitted.
pub const OFFICE_CLOSING_TIME_IS_NOT_PUBLISHED : bool = true;
pub const OFFICE_CLOSING_NOTE : str = "data/fleet_seed.json:299 publishes an opening time and no closing time, and no other file in this repository states one. window_start_is_allowed therefore has a floor and no ceiling: a start late in the evening is not refused by it, and that is an unjudged case rather than a published promise. RE-MEASURED 2026-09-21, because the sentence above was first measured when specs/ held 12 tracked files and it holds 43 now: a case-insensitive search over .rs, .json, .sql, .t27 and .md for office_close, closing_time, closes_at and closing hour returns this file's own two lines and nothing else, so the count of sources is still zero and is now dated twice. NARROWED 2026-09-21 after the brain read. This note used to end what is missing is the owner's closing hour, and that has become half wrong: the owner HAS named a time, and it is a DELIVERY CUT-OFF rather than a closing hour. It is declared below as DELIVERY_CUTOFF_MIN and it deliberately does not become a ceiling on this function. What is still missing is the hour the office itself shuts, which nothing publishes.";

; --- The delivery cut-off, and why it is not a ceiling ---------------------------------------------
; NEW ON 2026-09-21, and it is the OWNER'S WORD rather than a measurement.
; brain:business_rules:818-820, rule 3 of the five the owner named on 2026-08-20: deliveries and
; collections run until 17:30, and PAST 17:30 THE BOT DOES NOT DECIDE FOR ITSELF -- it says the time
; must be agreed and hands the question to a human, offering neither a refusal nor a promise of its
; own. brain:business_rules:798-804 states what kind of rule that is: the five are the owner's
; decisions, they are not checked against data and they do not cancel any measurement; the figures
; standing beside them are the grounds for deciding, not the source of the rule.
; WHAT IT CLOSED. brain:business_rules:763-769 had recorded working hours as an open gap, and noted
; that live refusals to drive were by TIME and not by geography. The live reply it quotes says the
; shop works until 18:00 -- 1080 minutes, not 1050. That is a MEASURED observation from one manager
; in the dialog corpus and the owner's 17:30 is the RULE; they are different kinds of fact about the
; same afternoon and the rule governs. The observed figure is declared so that a reader who meets it
; in the corpus recognises it instead of treating it as a correction.
pub const DELIVERY_CUTOFF_MIN : i32 = 1050;
pub const DELIVERY_CUTOFF_DECIDED_AT : str = "2026-08-20";
pub const DELIVERY_CUTOFF_IS_THE_OWNERS_WORD : bool = true;
pub const CUTOFF_COVERS_COLLECTIONS_TOO : bool = true;
pub const OBSERVED_LATE_REFUSAL_HOUR_MIN : i32 = 1080;
pub const THE_OBSERVED_HOUR_IS_NOT_THE_RULE : bool = true;
; THREE THINGS THE CUT-OFF IS NOT, each one a wrong turn that would have been easy to take.
; (a) It is not an office closing hour. The office may well stand open past the last delivery, and
;     nothing published says when it shuts, so CLOSING_TIME_SOURCES_FOUND stays 0 and
;     OFFICE_CLOSING_TIME_IS_NOT_PUBLISHED stays true. Folding the two together would publish an
;     hour the owner never gave.
; (b) It is not a refusal. The owner's rule is explicit that past the cut-off the bot must not
;     decide -- not refuse and not promise -- so making window_start_is_allowed return false after
;     1050 would have the machine do exactly what the rule forbids, and it would do it in the
;     direction that costs the shop a booking. window_start_is_allowed and delivery_destination_ready
;     are therefore UNCHANGED by this section: every assert about them below reads as it did before.
;     The third outcome lives in its own function and a consumer must branch on it.
; (c) It is not known to bound a window's END. The rule is written about deliveries and collections
;     themselves -- the handover -- and the brain does not say whether a two-hour window may START
;     before 17:30 and finish after it. The narrow reading is taken and the gap is declared: a window
;     that starts inside the cut-off and ends outside it is not judged here at all.
pub const THE_CUTOFF_IS_NOT_AN_OFFICE_CLOSING_HOUR : bool = true;
pub const PAST_CUTOFF_IS_ESCALATED_NOT_REFUSED : bool = true;
pub const PAST_CUTOFF_IS_NOT_PROMISED_EITHER : bool = true;
pub const THE_CUTOFF_IS_NOT_A_CEILING_ON_WINDOW_START_IS_ALLOWED : bool = true;
pub const WHETHER_THE_CUTOFF_BOUNDS_START_OR_END_IS_UNPUBLISHED : bool = true;
pub const CUTOFF_NOTE : str = "brain:business_rules:818-820 is the owner's rule of 2026-08-20: deliveries and collections run until 17:30, and past 17:30 the bot does not decide for itself -- it says the time must be agreed and passes the question to a human, giving neither a refusal nor a promise. That is a THIRD OUTCOME and it is modelled as one. window_start_is_allowed keeps a floor and no ceiling, exactly as before, because a ceiling there would refuse late windows and the rule forbids refusing them; window_start_needs_a_human answers the new question separately and a consumer must branch on it rather than read a boolean allowed/not-allowed. The brain does not say whether the cut-off bounds a window's start or its end, so the narrow reading is taken: a window starting after 1050 needs a human, and a window that starts before it and ends after it is unjudged here. Nothing in this file is an office closing hour, which remains unpublished.";

; --- THE PUBLISHED TARIFF LADDER ------------------------------------------------------------------
; UNTIL 2026-09-21 THIS SECTION WAS A REFUSAL, and the refusal is kept in view because it was TRUE
; WHEN WRITTEN. It declared exactly one area fee -- the seed's Bangtao row -- and made the LENGTH
; of that one-element array the prohibition: "One published area fee, and the array length is the
; thing that says so. A second row cannot arrive without this assert being deleted deliberately."
; Every sentence inside it still checks out against the source it was measured on.
; data/fleet_seed.json:306 still carries one delivery figure, :307 still sets tariff_table to null,
; and :308 still forbids interpolating any other area's price from that figure.
; WHAT WAS WRONG WAS THE UNIVERSE, NOT THE ARITHMETIC. The seed is not the shop. The owner's
; knowledge base publishes a tariff ladder BY DISTRICT, it published one long before this file was
; written, and this file had never gone to look. A refusal that searches the wrong place is not
; made honest by every line of the search being correct -- which is the whole reason the counts
; below name the corpus they were taken over.
; --
; THE SOURCE IS NOT A FILE IN THIS REPOSITORY, SO IT IS CITED IN A DIFFERENT FORM. The owner's
; knowledge base ("the brain") is a set of live nodes on the server, not tracked content here.
; A citation into it is written brain:<node>:<line> -- for example brain:booking_flow:74-92 -- and
; it can never be resolved by a path in this tree. That is deliberate: a reader must not mistake a
; brain line for a repository line and conclude the gate checks it. It does not. Read-only dumps of
; the nodes were taken and read on 2026-09-21; the nodes are Russian and every rule below is a
; TRANSLATION, never a transcription (L3 forbids Cyrillic in a spec, including inside a comment).
; District names follow the transliteration data/fleet_seed.json already uses -- "Bangtao",
; "Kamala", "Thalang", one word, no spaces -- and "Maikhao" is formed the same way.
pub const BRAIN_SOURCE_KIND : str = "owner knowledge base, not a file in this repository";
pub const BRAIN_CITATION_FORM : str = "brain:<node>:<line>";
pub const BRAIN_READ_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const BRAIN_LADDER_NODE_FLOW : str = "brain:booking_flow:74-92";
pub const BRAIN_LADDER_NODE_RULES : str = "brain:business_rules:626-645";
pub const BRAIN_LADDER_SOURCE_COUNT : i32 = 2;
pub const BRAIN_CITATIONS_ARE_NOT_REPOSITORY_PATHS : bool = true;
pub const BRAIN_IS_NOT_CHECKED_BY_ANY_GATE_IN_THIS_REPOSITORY : bool = true;
pub const EVERY_BRAIN_RULE_HERE_IS_TRANSLATED_NOT_TRANSCRIBED : bool = true;
;
; TWO KINDS OF FACT, AND THE BRAIN IS CAREFUL TO SAY WHICH -- so this file is too, per row.
; A MEASURED fact is read off data. brain:business_rules:602-611 names the corpus exactly: 710
; de-identified client dialogs, 31245 messages, exactly one year from 2025-06-01 to 2026-05-31,
; ALL 710 parsed with no sampling, crosschecked 2026-08-20, and the node says in its own words that
; this is a MEASUREMENT AND NOT AN OWNER RULE -- numbers taken off correspondence do not overrule
; the owner's decisions. brain:business_rules:621-623 adds the freshness honestly: the corpus was
; snapshotted once on 2026-06-01 and has not been refreshed, so the months before the read are not
; in it and its seasonal figures belong to the 2025/26 season.
; An OWNER fact is the owner's word on a dated decision, and it outranks the measurement where the
; two meet. Both kinds are published; neither is a quote to a client (D11, below).
pub const FACT_MEASURED : u8 = 1;
pub const FACT_OWNER_WORD : u8 = 2;
pub const CORPUS_DIALOGS : i32 = 710;
pub const CORPUS_MESSAGES : i32 = 31245;
pub const CORPUS_FIRST_DAY : str = "2025-06-01";
pub const CORPUS_LAST_DAY : str = "2026-05-31";
pub const CORPUS_WAS_FULLY_PARSED_NOT_SAMPLED : bool = true;
pub const CORPUS_CROSSCHECKED_AT : str = "2026-08-20";
pub const CORPUS_SNAPSHOT_IS_NOT_REFRESHED : bool = true;
pub const A_MEASUREMENT_DOES_NOT_OVERRULE_THE_OWNER : bool = true;
;
; THE LADDER. brain:booking_flow:74-92 lists the districts with a mention count beside each;
; brain:business_rules:626-634 confirms the same ladder INDEPENDENTLY, off the same corpus but by a
; different count, and adds the sentence that makes it a rule rather than a table: the step depends
; on the DISTRICT of Phuket and on nothing else.
; ORDER IS NOT RANK. Rows are grouped by step, and within the 290 step Bangtao is first so that the
; seed's one documented row sits at ladder index 0 and the two index spaces cannot drift apart.
; Reading the order as popularity would be wrong; the mention counts are declared separately and
; Patong, not Bangtao, is the most mentioned.
pub const LADDER_DISTRICT_COUNT : i32 = 12;
pub const LADDER_DISTRICT_NAMES : [12]str = ["Bangtao", "Patong", "Surin", "Kamala", "Karon", "Kata", "Kathu", "Paklok", "Rawai", "Naiharn", "Chalong", "Maikhao"];
